## Known limitation while this workaround is active

Any `hermes <subcommand>` invoked from a normal shell sees no API-key credentials. `hermes status --all`, `hermes doctor`, `hermes config show`, `hermes chat`, `hermes send`, etc. all call `load_hermes_dotenv()` on `~/.hermes/.env` and find no secret-bearing vars there — because we deliberately moved them all to `.env.op`. The credentials only live in the **daemon's** process env (shim-injected at launch time). So `hermes doctor` will keep flagging "No API key found in `~/.hermes/.env`" and listing tools as missing credentials, and `hermes status --all` will show every "API Keys" row as `✗`, even though the daemon itself has the keys and is working.

The daemon is the only credentialed surface; CLI subcommands run from outside it are not. Treat that as a known cost of the workaround, not a separate bug to chase. Closes when upstream `#18734` lands and we can revert per Option B above.

## What this workaround does NOT change

- `~/.hermes/config.yaml` — untouched. Fallback chain, provider declarations, provider_routing block all unchanged.
- The launchd plist `~/Library/LaunchAgents/ai.hermes.gateway.plist` — untouched. Still invokes the shim as `ProgramArguments[0]` with the python command appended.
- 1Password vault contents — untouched. Same items, same fields, same op:// reference shape.
- Anything in `IDAHO-VAULT/` — untouched on the local-state side. The vault witness at `!/SIGNALS/TOUCHING-ME-TOUCHING-NOUS-2026-06-25.md` covers the upstream-contact arc; a brief follow-up note on `LAF-US/IDAHO-VAULT#690` records the workaround application.

